# review 20261031-010936/2: land after fixes by claude-opus-5-5
lib/agent-questions.sh:53: blocking: 2#1 waiting_questions checks a question with question_file_safe and then reads it again by name with head -n 1. It reads the session record the same way with an unbounded $(cat), and question_file_safe's own size check uses wc -c < path after a separate -L/-f test. A symlink swapped in by the agent between the check and the read is followed. agent-answer (bin/agent-answer:19-21) has the same pattern. — Reproduced: agent-status --wait-question printed the first line of a host-only file in 46/100 runs. Swapping in /dev/zero or a FIFO makes the host read without bound or block forever, so the waiter never wakes the facilitator. The brief requires refusing symlinks and non-regular files and capping reads; that only holds if each file is opened once and read with a byte cap from that same descriptor.
agents/agent-ask:52: should-fix: 2#2 On timeout agent-ask exits but leaves questions/<q>.md unanswered and unmarked. — A review session keeps running after a timeout, so the stale question keeps counting as waiting: agent-status lists it, agent-status --wait-question returns immediately on every call (which defeats waiting without tokens), and agent-answer reports success for an answer no one will read. Mark timed-out questions, for example with a <q>.timeout file, and exclude them on the host.
bin/agent-answer:44: nit: 2#3 The comment says noclobber means an existing file or symlink is never replaced. dash and bash noclobber still open an existing non-regular target, including through a symlink, without O_EXCL. — A symlink planted after the checks that points at a host-writable FIFO or device receives the answer text. The impact is low because the content is the host's own text, but the comment overstates the guarantee.
agents/agent-ask:13: nit: 2#4 Timeouts up to 21599 seconds are accepted, just under RuntimeMaxSec=6h. — The brief wants the wait well below the unit limit so a timed-out session can still take the NEEDS DECISION stop. A value near the limit lets the unit kill the session first. A lower cap would be safer.
